  <div id="content">
    <div id="welcome" style="color:#888;font-size:14px;padding:40px 20px;text-align:center;line-height:2">
      <div style="font-size:32px;margin-bottom:12px">📬</div>
      <strong>21 new proposals</strong> from the C2A2 hunt agents.<br>
      Click any item on the left to review it.<br>
      Use the buttons to Approve, Double-check, Change, or Deny each one.<br>
      When done, hit <strong>Send Decisions</strong> below.
    </div>
    
    <div class="prop-card" id="card-PROP-2026-06-17-002" style="display:none">
      <div style="background:#0a5a6a18;border-left:4px solid #0a5a6a;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#0a5a6a;letter-spacing:.05em">BERNARDO KASTRUP · INTERVIEW · 2026-05-21</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">A Conversation with Bernardo Kastrup on European AI Sovereignty (AI Meet-Up XL)</div>
        <a href="https://aiinnovationcenter.nl/a-conversation-with-bernardo-kastrup-on-european-ai-sovereignty/" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#0a5a6a;opacity:.8">https://aiinnovationcenter.nl/a-conversation-with-bernardo-kastrup-on-european-a…</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Speaking not as a philosopher but as founder/CEO of the chip company Euclyd, Kastrup argues Europe can still reach AI sovereignty only if it stops believing software alone will save it and reclaims control of AI hardware. He is blunt that "GPU is video game hardware... not designed from scratch for the way neural networks work," and frames Euclyd's bet (silicon and systems-in-package for AI workloads, ~$100M raised) as competing on agility over scale and on "a deeper understanding of intelligence and consciousness." Companion source: his IAI TV essay "Europe's last hope in the AI race."</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This is the same Kastrup who calls machine consciousness about as plausible as the Flying Spaghetti Monster — now publicly designing the hardware substrate of AI and explicitly citing his "understanding of intelligence and consciousness" as the design edge. It is a new, untracked dimension of the program: the practical/industrial face of analytic idealism, and a live test of how a consciousness-as-fundamental thinker draws the line between intelligence (which he builds for) and consciousness (which he denies machines have).</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #0a5a6a;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#7a1a1a;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">Speculative</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> Does an idealist who denies machine consciousness have anything distinctive to say about how AI should be built — or is his metaphysics idle with respect to engineering and policy?</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> Kastrup's Euclyd position — that current AI runs on repurposed "video game hardware," and that a correct, non-conflated understanding of *intelligence* (information processing) vs. *consciousness* (experience) is itself a competitive design and deployment principle</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> A demonstration that the intelligence/consciousness distinction central to analytic idealism is action-guiding outside philosophy: it underwrites both a hardware thesis (design for how neural nets actually compute, not for graphics) and a values thesis ("European values need to be reflected in how we deploy AI")</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: Per event reporting (AI Meet-Up XL, May 21 2026; Dispatches Europe; IAI TV). Primary recording was NOT retrieved (the AI Innovation Center page would not render text to the fetcher); claims above are grounded in multiple secondary reports of Kastrup's own statements, not in the primary transcript. Flagged Speculative accordingly.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #0a5a6a;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 2 — <span style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">Medium</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> C2A2 treats "can AI agents be genuine tradition-members" as partly a consciousness question; but Kastrup's industrial work suggests a parallel, separable question — what can highly capable *non-conscious* intelligence do inside a tradition?</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> Kastrup's operative split — he builds for machine intelligence while denying machine consciousness, treating the two as cleanly separable in practice</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> A sharpening for C2A2: the AI-membership question can be decomposed into (a) a capability question (intelligence — Kastrup is bullish) and (b) a participation question (consciousness/experience — Kastrup is skeptical), letting the network reason about each independently</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: Kastrup's long-standing public skepticism about machine consciousness combined with his active investment in machine intelligence; consistent with Kastrup PRS-22 (AI can scaffold/represent but not occupy the experiential pole).</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:12px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CROSS-TRADITION SIGNALS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444">Direct C2A2-relevant AI signal — flagged strongly per agent watch-list. Strong tie to **McGilchrist PRS-22 / PRS-18** (AI as left-hemisphere processing without right-hemisphere participation): Kastrup's intelligence/consciousness split is the analytic-idealist twin of McGilchrist's simulation/participation split, and both land on "build/allow the capability, withhold the participatory status." Also bears on **Hoffman** (conscious realism vs. Kastrup on what machines could instantiate) and on the network's own infrastructure choices. Note the cross-thinker convergence: Koch's "Can AI be conscious?" (Essentia, 2026-04-03) reaches a similar no via IIT, giving three independent routes (hemispheric, idealist, IIT) to the same demarcation.</div></div>
      </div>
