    <div class="prop-card" id="card-PROP-2026-06-24-001" style="display:none">
      <div style="background:#3a3a6a18;border-left:4px solid #3a3a6a;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#3a3a6a;letter-spacing:.05em">IAIN MCGILCHRIST · INTERVIEW · 2026-06-04</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Iain McGilchrist and Charles Eisenstein in conversation – Being in the World Today</div>
        <a href="https://galileocommission.org/iain-mcgilchrist-and-charles-eisenstein-in-conversation-being-in-the-world-today/" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#3a3a6a;opacity:.8">https://galileocommission.org/iain-mcgilchrist-and-charles-eisenstein-in-convers…</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">A 90-minute Galileo Commission dialogue (recorded 4 June 2026) between McGilchrist and Charles Eisenstein, growing out of McGilchrist's Oxford talk at "The Good, The Beautiful, and The True." McGilchrist argues that the essential role of education is teaching people *how to be in the world* — that fundamental values are intrinsic rather than instrumental, and that our capacity to embody those values constitutes who we essentially are. He links "truth" to the older sense of *treu* (being true to oneself, loyal to others), and the two discuss how to act with agency and centredness rather than passivity in the face of personal and collective challenge.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This shifts McGilchrist's program from cultural *diagnosis* (the left-hemisphere takeover) toward a constructive *pedagogy of being* — answering the open question logged 2026-06-07 about what hemispheric rebalancing looks like at the level of the person, not just the institution. "Being before doing" is the right-hemisphere ground stated as an educational aim.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #3a3a6a;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#1a6b3c;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">High</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> McGilchrist's hemispheric program has specified rebalancing institutionally (the Sheldonian/Ralston settings, PRS-29/36) but not what right-hemisphere primacy asks of an individual person navigating daily collective crisis.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> McGilchrist's claim that the primary task of education is teaching *how to be in the world* — that fundamental values are intrinsic (not instrumental) and that embodying them constitutes the self; truth as *treu*, fidelity to oneself and others.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> A pedagogy of *being before doing*: orienting from inner state and embodied value rather than instrumental problem-solving, which is the right hemisphere's mode rendered as a teachable disposition rather than a diagnosis.</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: "Iain spoke about being in the world – how to be in the world – as the essential role of education... He argued that fundamental values are intrinsic rather than instrumental, and that our capacity to embody these values constitutes who we essentially are. He also reminded us that the word truth is related to 'treu', being true to oneself, and loyal to others."</div>
        </div>
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #3a3a6a;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 2 — <span style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">Medium</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> How to sustain agency without succumbing to passivity or hopelessness in a culture of "progressive enshittification" — i.e., how the right-hemisphere stance survives contact with a degrading left-hemisphere world.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> The McGilchrist–Eisenstein joint framing of cultivating inner life *and* corresponding outer engagement ("what is ours to do?") as a single integrated movement rather than a choice between withdrawal and activism.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> A both/and posture — inner centredness as the precondition for, not the alternative to, effective outer action — modelling how a mature member of the tradition holds attention under cultural pressure.</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: "how we can navigate a world of progressive 'enshittification', cultivating both our inner life and a corresponding outer engagement in the world. We all have to ask: what is ours to do?"</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:12px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CROSS-TRADITION SIGNALS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444">Strong C2A2 pedagogy signal: McGilchrist frames the *formation of a mature person within a tradition* as the central educational act — directly relevant to the second-first-language / curriculum-of-maturity goal at the heart of the accelerator. The "intrinsic vs. instrumental value" claim resonates with **[[07_stump_agent]]** Stump's Thomistic account of the good as constitutive (and Aquinas on intrinsic finality) and with **[[06_fredrickson_agent]]** Fredrickson's intrinsic-flourishing distinction. The "being before doing" emphasis converges with **[[11_kastrup_agent]]** Kastrup's June 2026 "life is not a test but a journey of self-discovery" line — flag the McGilchrist/Kastrup convergence on *being over achieving* as especially significant given their close collaboration. The Eisenstein "transition of civilization's defining mythology" theme touches the tradition-vs-tradition / paradigm-shift axis.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:12px 18px 18px;border-top:1px solid #eee;display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap">
