    <div class="prop-card" id="card-PROP-2026-06-25-004" style="display:none">
      <div style="background:#a0306a18;border-left:4px solid #a0306a;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#a0306a;letter-spacing:.05em">BARBARA FREDRICKSON · PAPER · 2025</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Positively in-sync: Convergent validity across three distinct assessments of the positive affective quality of social interaction</div>
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/02654075251359408" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#a0306a;opacity:.8">https://doi.org/10.1177/02654075251359408</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Zhou, West, Berman & Fredrickson (Journal of Social and Personal Relationships, 2025) test whether three distinct ways of assessing the positive affective quality of a social interaction — self-report, perceived positivity resonance, and behavioral coding — converge on the same underlying construct. Establishing convergent validity across these methods strengthens the measurement foundation of positivity resonance.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This is a measurement-infrastructure paper, and the wiki's Fredrickson holdings are mostly substantive findings rather than methodology. For C2A2's own measurement ambitions, the question "do different instruments for the affective quality of an interaction agree?" is load-bearing: it tells us whether resonance can be measured robustly across self-report, perceived, and behavioral channels — which is precisely the multi-channel measurement problem C2A2 faces when instrumenting tradition-dialogue encounters.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #a0306a;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">Medium</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> The positive affective quality of an interaction is assessed in incompatible ways across studies (self-report vs. perceived positivity resonance vs. observer behavioral coding), leaving open whether these methods measure one construct or several.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> A convergent-validity study comparing three distinct assessments of interaction positivity on the same interactions.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> Demonstrated convergence across self-report, perceived-resonance, and behavioral measures, supporting positivity resonance as a single construct measurable through multiple channels and licensing mixed-method assessment.</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: The paper's design and aim — establishing convergent validity across three distinct assessments of the positive affective quality of social interaction (J. Social and Personal Relationships, 2025).</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:12px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CROSS-TRADITION SIGNALS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444">- **C2A2 measurement framework:** directly relevant to Measurement Levels 1–2 — if resonance is robust across self-report, perceived, and behavioral instruments, those become candidate cross-validated metrics for community-dialogue quality, not just lab dyads.
- **Friston:** convergence of subjective, perceived, and behavioral readouts is what one expects if all three sample a shared latent coupling state between agents.
- **Hawkins:** multi-channel agreement as evidence the construct has a stable underlying (neuro)behavioral referent.
- **Loughran/C2A2 method:** a measurement-robustness precedent worth citing when defending the falsifiability/quantification claims in the measurement-prototype and coil-falsifier work.</div></div>
      </div>
