    <div class="prop-card" id="card-PROP-2026-06-25-001" style="display:none">
      <div style="background:#a0306a18;border-left:4px solid #a0306a;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#a0306a;letter-spacing:.05em">BARBARA FREDRICKSON · PAPER · 2025</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Does positivity resonance signify love? Markers of positivity resonance in long-term married couples relate to trait and state love</div>
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/pere.70008" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#a0306a;opacity:.8">https://doi.org/10.1111/pere.70008</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Lai, Otero, Chen, Wells, Levenson & Fredrickson test whether behavioral markers of positivity resonance (shared positive affect plus behavioral synchrony) actually track love itself in long-term married couples. Using the Berkeley long-term-marriage dyadic-interaction data, they find that observed markers of positivity resonance relate to both trait love (enduring orientation toward the partner) and state love (in-the-moment felt love during interaction).</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This is the validation paper the positivity-resonance program needed: it closes the loop between the *behavioral signature* Fredrickson operationalizes (synchrony, shared positive affect) and the *construct* it is meant to index (love). It moves "positivity resonance = the basic unit of love" from theoretical definition toward empirically anchored claim, using observational data from a different lab (Levenson's) than the one that coined the construct — a cross-lab convergence the wiki has not yet captured.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #a0306a;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#1a6b3c;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">High</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> Positivity resonance is *defined* as the micro-level constituent of love, but whether its observable behavioral markers actually correspond to felt love (rather than mere pleasant interaction) had not been empirically demonstrated in enduring real relationships.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> A marker-based observational paradigm applied to long-term married couples, relating coded positivity-resonance behaviors to independently measured trait love and state love.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> Behavioral markers of positivity resonance predict both trait and state love, providing convergent empirical support that positivity resonance is a valid behavioral signature of love rather than a definitional stipulation.</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: The paper's central finding — markers of positivity resonance in long-term married couples relate to both trait and state love (Personal Relationships, 2025).</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:12px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CROSS-TRADITION SIGNALS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444">- **Stump:** Stump's account of love as the will of the good for the other plus desire for union has a possible empirical correlate here — positivity resonance as the behavioral trace of the union-desiring component. A live bridge for the "is love in both frameworks structurally the same phenomenon?" question flagged in the Stump↔Fredrickson watch-list.
- **Friston:** dyadic synchrony as a marker of mutual prediction / shared generative model between partners.
- **Hawkins/Levenson neuroscience:** behavioral-synchrony markers as observable correlates of inter-brain coupling.
- **C2A2 relevance:** if a behavioral signature reliably indexes genuine connection, it is a candidate measurable proxy for resonance in tradition-dialogue encounters, not only intimate dyads.</div></div>
      </div>
