      <div style="background:#55518;border-left:4px solid #555;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#555;letter-spacing:.05em">RICHARD ROHR · BLOG · 2026-06-28</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Called and Sent (Daily Meditation, opening Week 26 'Everyone Is Chosen')</div>
        <a href="https://cac.org/daily-meditations/called-and-sent/" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#555;opacity:.8">https://cac.org/daily-meditations/called-and-sent/</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Opening the new "Everyone Is Chosen" series, Rohr reads Romans 11 to redefine chosenness. Election is "definitive and irrevocable" (Rom 11:29), yet "has nothing to do with worthiness… God's choice has to do with God alone, not with us being worthy or ready. No one is ever ready! In fact, the readiness comes from experiencing and surrendering to the chosenness." Chosenness is "for the sake of experiencing mercy" (Rom 11:30–31): not so the elect "can feel superior and saved" — "immature religion always stops" there — but so they "know what it feels like to be God's beloved… to be gazed at with the gaze of God, and to be mirrored by the ultimate mirror," and can then "communicate that chosenness to everybody else." The biblical arc "begins with chosenness for a few, but it always moves toward egalitarian chosenness for everyone," and only those who have "first experienced that boundless abundance in themselves" can convey it. Rohr adds that the creature's inability to love God back as fully as it is loved "keeps us in the realm of desire — always yearning… It keeps us humble and honest."</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This is the most direct recent statement of Rohr's reframing of *election* — the doctrine most prone to elitism — as a relational role conferred for the sake of its own universalization. It supplies the contemplative twin of Wright's Romans/covenant theology and lands squarely on the Summa 2026 central theme: perspective-limited agents individuated precisely so that loving unity can be mediated outward. The Rohr wiki carries the *Universal Christ* as cosmic Christology but lacks a node on **chosenness/election as individuation-for-unity** — the move from "chosen-few" to "chosen-all." The "ultimate mirror / gazed-at" language is also a citable contemplative formulation of consciousness-first metaphysics.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #555;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">Medium</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> How can election (chosenness of a few) be reconciled with universal/egalitarian belovedness without collapsing into elitism?</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> Rohr's reading of Romans 11 — chosenness irrevocable but not merit-based, given "for the sake of experiencing mercy," and only real when communicated outward</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> Election is a relational role-conferral whose telos is its own universalization; an agent is singled out ("chosen") precisely so it can mediate chosenness to all, so individuation serves loving unity rather than separating from it — the arc runs from chosen-few to chosen-all</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: "begins with chosenness for a few, but it always moves toward egalitarian chosenness for everyone"; readiness "comes from experiencing and surrendering to the chosenness"
```
```
PRS-CANDIDATE-B:</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:12px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CROSS-TRADITION SIGNALS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444">- **Wright Agent**: Direct scriptural twin, and a clean same-week pairing — Wright is independently in the Romans material this week (PROP-2026-06-28-001). Wright's reading of Israel's irrevocable calling and "salvation = inclusion in the covenanted people" is the second-temple-disciplined statement of Rohr's chosen-few→chosen-all arc. Recommend the two same-day proposals be cross-linked as a Wright↔Rohr Romans/peoplehood bridge.
- **Friston Agent**: "To be mirrored by the ultimate mirror" and "readiness comes from… surrendering to the chosenness" read naturally as generative-model alignment — belovedness as a high-precision prior the agent does not earn but receives and then propagates; surrender as precision-letting-go. Speculative but tractable.
- **Hoffman / Kastrup Agents**: The "gaze of God / ultimate mirror" is a contemplative formulation of consciousness-first metaphysics — the agent constituted by being known/mirrored by a primary awareness, not by self-grounding. Useful internal source for the mind-monism locus.
- **C2A2 master / paradigm flags**: Flag as **paradigm-bridge candidate** — "chosenness-as-individuation-for-unity" is among the cleanest theological articulations yet of the Summa central theme (perspective-limited agents individuated so that loving unity is the telos). Recommend master-agent cross-link with the Rohr Universal-Christ node, the Wright peoplehood node, and the same-day Wright proposal.</div></div>
