        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#555;letter-spacing:.05em">RICHARD ROHR · BLOG · 2026-06-27</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Hope in Hard Times: Weekly Summary (Daily Meditation, Week 25)</div>
        <a href="https://cac.org/daily-meditations/hope-in-hard-times-weekly-summary/" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#555;opacity:.8">https://cac.org/daily-meditations/hope-in-hard-times-weekly-summary/</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This weekly summary curates a convergent, multi-voice account of hope as something other than optimism about outcomes. Rohr (Wednesday): "Hope is a participation in the very life of God. It has nothing to do with circumstances or events going well. It can even thrive in the midst of adversity." Bourgeault: hope "is a quality of aliveness… it lies at the beginning, as a pulse of truth that sends us forth," not a feeling that results from a happy outcome. Batiste: hope is "the language of the invisible… just as real, if not more real, than the things we can see." Kaba: hope is "a discipline… practiced every day." McLaren sharpens the logic: "If we can see a likely path to our desired outcome, we have hope; if we can see no possible path… we have despair. When our prime motive is love, a different logic comes into play. We find courage and confidence, not in the likelihood of a good outcome, but in our commitment to love." The week's practice (Luther E. Smith Jr., via Howard Thurman's "I Will Sing a New Song") frames singing as embodied hope: "Do you have a tune that carries you?"</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">The week states, in contemplative register, a precise computational claim the C2A2 network cares about: hope and despair are usually functions of a perceived *path* to preferred states — and love reorders that function. This is the cleanest recent bridge between Rohr's contemplative tradition and the active-inference / affective-science programs in the network, and it is high-value for Summa 2026 loci on hope as a theological virtue (II-II Q.17–22) and on the contemplative life under adversity ("salvation in fear and trembling"). The Rohr wiki has no node on **hope-as-participation-not-prediction** or on the love-reframing of action under apparently unreachable goals.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #555;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">Medium</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> Is hope a forecast about outcomes (and so defeated by bad odds), or something structurally different?</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> The week's convergent claim (Rohr/Bourgeault/Batiste) that hope is "participation in the very life of God," a "pulse of truth that sends us forth," independent of circumstances</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> Decouple hope from expected-outcome estimation; reframe it as a love-grounded orientation/stance that can thrive under adverse priors — "a quality of aliveness" prior to results rather than a feeling produced by them</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: Rohr's "nothing to do with circumstances… can thrive in the midst of adversity"; Bourgeault's hope at "the beginning, not the end"
