      <div style="background:#55518;border-left:4px solid #555;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#555;letter-spacing:.05em">N.T. WRIGHT · INTERVIEW · 2026-06</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Capital Conversations — N.T. Wright on Women in Ministry, Faith, and Scripture</div>
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qEpZHaP11wQ" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#555;opacity:.8">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qEpZHaP11wQ</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">In this Capital Conversations interview (released mid-June 2026, circulating widely after the Southern Baptist Convention's vote to bar churches with women pastors), Wright makes his historical-exegetical case that Scripture supports women in public ministry. His central move is reconstructive rather than proof-texting: when Paul finished Romans, he entrusted the letter not to a "senior apostle" but to Phoebe, "an independent businesswoman who lives just east of Corinth" (Cenchreae), traveling to Rome — so "the high probability is that the first person to do public exposition of Paul's letter to the Romans was a businesswoman from east of Corinth." His second exhibit is Mary Magdalene, commissioned as the first witness and announcer of the resurrection. Wright reads the restrictive texts (e.g., 1 Timothy 2) as locally contextual, not as a universal ban, and frames ministry roles as conferred by call, gift, and commission within the body.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This is a fresh primary statement (past ~30 days) of how interpretive and proclamatory authority is actually distributed in the earliest "people of God" — the load-bearing C2A2 unit. The Wright wiki carries his peoplehood-salvation and corporate-substance material but has no node on the *internal differentiation of the body* — who carries, reads, expounds, and announces the foundational texts and events, and on what basis. Phoebe-as-first-expositor-of-Romans and Mary-Magdalene-as-first-resurrection-herald give a citable, second-temple-disciplined account of role-individuation-by-commission inside one corporate entity. It is directly relevant to Summa 2026 loci on holy orders, the diversity of charisms in one body, and the church as a real composite agent, and it pairs cleanly with the same-day Rohr "chosenness" proposal (PROP-2026-06-28-002) on the move from chosen-few to chosen-all.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #555;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">Medium</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> Within the people of God, what confers authority to carry, read, and publicly expound the foundational texts — formal hierarchy, gender, or something else?</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> Wright's historical reconstruction of Phoebe as the bearer of Romans and, by high probability, its first public expositor in Rome</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> Interpretive authority in the earliest church was distributed by function and commission, not by office or gender; the bearer-expositor role is constitutive of the people-of-God as a real interpretive community, not an honorary add-on</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: Wright's claim that Paul gave Romans to "a businesswoman from east of Corinth" who most likely read and explained it aloud to the Roman church
