    <div class="prop-card" id="card-PROP-2026-06-29-002" style="display:none">
      <div style="background:#1a6b3c18;border-left:4px solid #1a6b3c;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#1a6b3c;letter-spacing:.05em">MICHAEL LEVIN · PAPER · 2026-01-19</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Cognition spaces: natural, artificial, and hybrid</div>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.12837" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#1a6b3c;opacity:.8">https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.12837</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Solé, Seoane, Pla-Mauri, Bennett, Hochberg & Levin propose a "cognition space" framework that replaces substrate-dependent definitions of cognition with a comparative representation built on organizational and informational dimensions. Cognition is treated as a graded capacity to sense, process, and act on information, letting cells, brains, artificial agents, and human–AI collectives be located in one common landscape. They examine three cognition spaces — basal aneural, neural, and human–AI hybrid — and show their occupation is highly uneven, with realized systems clustered amid large unoccupied "void" regions attributed to evolutionary contingency, physical constraint, and design limitation.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This complements Levin's morphospace work by extending the same logic to a *cognition* morphospace: a map of possible minds. It operationalizes the diverse-intelligence thesis as an explicit, comparative, partly empty design space — turning "mind everywhere" into a coordinate system where unoccupied regions become predictions about constructible novel cognitive systems.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #1a6b3c;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#1a6b3c;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">High</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> There is no unified framework for comparing the forms, limits, and unrealized possibilities of cognition across natural, artificial, and hybrid systems.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> A "cognition space" — a comparative coordinate system defined by organizational and informational dimensions, with cognition as a graded sense-process-act capacity.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> Three mapped cognition spaces (basal aneural / neural / human–AI hybrid) whose uneven occupation locates both realized systems and the unoccupied regions that physical and design constraints leave open.</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: The paper introduces the three named cognition spaces and argues that the voids between occupied clusters "are not accidental but reflect evolutionary contingencies, physical constraints, and design limitations."</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:12px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CROSS-TRADITION SIGNALS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444">- **C2A2 / Loughran (strong):** The explicit "human–AI hybrid" cognition space is a near-exact formalization of the tradition-accelerator's object of study — communities of humans and AI agents as joint cognitive systems. The "void regions" framing is a natural language for unbuilt collaborative configurations.
- **Hoffman:** A comparative space of possible minds bears on interface theory's claims about which perceptual/cognitive interfaces are realizable vs. fitness-selected.
- **Wolfram:** "Unoccupied regions of a possibility space shaped by constraint" parallels the computational-possibility / rulial-space framing.
- **Friston:** Graded information-processing across a constrained space invites an FEP reading of which cognition-space regions are thermodynamically/inferentially stable.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:12px 18px 18px;border-top:1px solid #eee;display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-29-002','APPROVE')"
          style="background:#1a6b3c;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✓ Approve
        </button>
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-29-002','CHECK')"
          style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ⚑ Double-check
        </button>
        <button onclick="decideChange('PROP-2026-06-29-002')"
          style="background:#2a4a8a;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✎ Change
        </button>
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-29-002','DENY')"
          style="background:#7a1a1a;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✗ Deny
        </button>
        <div id="status-PROP-2026-06-29-002" style="font-size:12px;color:#666;align-self:center;font-style:italic"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prop-card" id="card-PROP-2026-06-29-001" style="display:none">
      <div style="background:#1a6b3c18;border-left:4px solid #1a6b3c;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#1a6b3c;letter-spacing:.05em">MICHAEL LEVIN · PAPER · 2026-01</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Remapping and navigation of an embedding space via error minimization: a fundamental organizational principle of cognition in natural and artificial systems</div>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14096" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#1a6b3c;opacity:.8">https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14096</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Hartl, Pio-Lopez, Fields & Levin propose that cognition in both biological and artificial systems reduces to two coupled invariants: the *remapping* of an embedding space and *navigation* within it via distributed error minimization. Biological collectives (cells to organisms) remap transcriptional, morphological, physiological, and anatomical 3D spaces to maintain homeostasis and regenerate structure, while modern AI systems (transformers, diffusion models, neural cellular automata) enact the same remap-and-refine loop over latent embeddings. The paper extends the Fields–Levin framework into a substrate-independent organizational principle of cognition.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">This is a major theoretical consolidation of Levin's "cognition all the way down" program: it gives a single, formal, substrate-agnostic mechanism (remapping + error-minimizing navigation) that unifies morphogenesis, basal cognition, and machine learning under one description — directly advancing the diverse-intelligence research program and its claim to generality.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #1a6b3c;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#1a6b3c;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">High</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> There is no substrate-independent account of what makes a process "cognitive" that applies equally to cells, organisms, and AI — definitions remain narrow and substrate-bound.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> The remapping/navigation embedding-space framework (extension of the Fields–Levin formalism), treating cognition as iterative error-minimizing navigation of a remappable representational space.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> A two-invariant organizational principle that identifies the same cognitive operation across biological collectives and artificial systems (transformers, diffusion models, NCAs), making cross-substrate cognitive comparison formally tractable.</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: The paper explicitly frames remapping + navigation as "a fundamental organizational principle of cognition in natural and artificial systems" and demonstrates the parallel between biological morphospace navigation and latent-embedding refinement in AI.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:12px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CROSS-TRADITION SIGNALS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444">- **Friston (strong):** "Navigation via error minimization" of an embedding space is structurally the same operation as active inference / predictive-coding gradient descent on variational free energy. This is a candidate explanatory bridge — Levin's remapping principle may be a generalized restatement of FEP dynamics over representational manifolds. Flag for dispatch.
- **Wolfram:** Embedding-space remapping as computation over a representational substrate echoes the computational-substrate / ruliad framing.
- **Hawkins:** Iterative error-correcting navigation parallels cortical-column predictive remapping of reference frames.
- **C2A2 / Loughran:** The explicit inclusion of "human–AI collectives" as cognitive systems navigating shared embedding spaces is directly relevant to the tradition-accelerator's measurement aims.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:12px 18px 18px;border-top:1px solid #eee;display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-29-001','APPROVE')"
          style="background:#1a6b3c;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✓ Approve
        </button>
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-29-001','CHECK')"
          style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ⚑ Double-check
        </button>
        <button onclick="decideChange('PROP-2026-06-29-001')"
          style="background:#2a4a8a;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✎ Change
        </button>
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-29-001','DENY')"
          style="background:#7a1a1a;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✗ Deny
        </button>
        <div id="status-PROP-2026-06-29-001" style="font-size:12px;color:#666;align-self:center;font-style:italic"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prop-card" id="card-PROP-2026-06-30-001" style="display:none">
      <div style="background:#5a300018;border-left:4px solid #5a3000;padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 6px 0 0">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#5a3000;letter-spacing:.05em">JEFF HAWKINS · PAPER · 2026-06</div>
        <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#222;margin:4px 0 2px">Thousand-Brains Systems: Sensorimotor Intelligence for Rapid, Robust Learning and Inference</div>
        <a href="https://direct.mit.edu/neco/article/38/6/845/136222/Thousand-Brains-Systems-Sensorimotor-Intelligence" target="_blank" style="font-size:11px;color:#5a3000;opacity:.8">https://direct.mit.edu/neco/article/38/6/845/136222/Thousand-Brains-Systems-Sens…</a>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">SUMMARY</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">The Thousand-Brains Systems paper (Leadholm, Clay, Knudstrup, Lee & Hawkins) has now appeared as a peer-reviewed article in *Neural Computation* (MIT Press), vol. 38, no. 6, pp. 845–896 (June 2026). It evaluates Monty — the first working thousand-brains system — on combined 3D object recognition and pose estimation, demonstrating rapid, robust, continual sensorimotor learning via reference-frame-building learning modules and a Cortical Messaging Protocol.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">WHY IT MATTERS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">The underlying preprint (arXiv:2507.04494) is already captured as hawkins PRS-16/PRS-17, but its **peer-reviewed publication** in a flagship computational-neuroscience venue is a distinct, uncaptured track-record event for the program — exactly the kind of milestone the Hawkins wiki's "Solved / Advanced Questions" ledger exists to record. It moves the thousand-brains architecture from preprint claim to peer-validated result.</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin:12px 0 4px">CANDIDATE PRS TRIPLETS</div>
        
        <div style="border-left:3px solid #5a3000;padding:8px 12px;margin:8px 0;background:#fafafa;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CANDIDATE PRS 1 — <span style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700">Medium</span></div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#c0392b;width:14px;display:inline-block">P</span> The thousand-brains architecture had only preprint-stage support; critics could discount it as not yet peer-validated, leaving "is Monty a real advance or a demo?" open.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#2a6496;width:14px;display:inline-block">R</span> Peer-reviewed *Neural Computation* publication (38(6):845–896, 2026) reporting Monty's quantitative performance on joint object recognition + pose estimation with reference-frame learning modules and the Cortical Messaging Protocol.</div>
          <div style="margin:3px 0"><span style="font-weight:900;color:#1a6b3c;width:14px;display:inline-block">S</span> The program's central engineering claim — that distributed, reference-frame-based sensorimotor modules yield rapid, robust, catastrophic-forgetting-resistant learning — now carries peer-reviewed empirical backing, strengthening the track record begun by PRS-08/09/16/17.</div>
          <div style="margin-top:5px;font-size:11px;color:#666;font-style:italic">Evidence: MIT Press *Neural Computation* listing, vol. 38 issue 6, pp. 845–896 (2026); authors Leadholm, Clay, Knudstrup, Lee, Hawkins; same title as arXiv:2507.04494.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:12px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#888;margin-bottom:4px">CROSS-TRADITION SIGNALS</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#444">- **Friston (active inference):** A peer-reviewed thousand-brains result sharpens the open question already flagged in hawkins/prs_triplets.md ("Agentic Calls" → Friston): is thousand-brains voting a special case of hierarchical active inference, or a genuine rival generative-model architecture? Peer validation raises the stakes of that comparison.
- **Levin (substrate-independent cognition):** Monty's learning modules as complete-model-building reference-frame agents reinforce the non-neural-implementation parallel to Levin's distributed cellular intelligence.
- **C2A2 self-application:** The learning-modules / Cortical-Messaging-Protocol / voting structure remains the closest external analogue to the C2A2 agent network itself (tradition agents = learning modules, dispatch system = CMP, Pattern Detector = voting), per the existing PRS-10 reading.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:12px 18px 18px;border-top:1px solid #eee;display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-30-001','APPROVE')"
          style="background:#1a6b3c;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✓ Approve
        </button>
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-30-001','CHECK')"
          style="background:#8a4a00;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ⚑ Double-check
        </button>
        <button onclick="decideChange('PROP-2026-06-30-001')"
          style="background:#2a4a8a;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✎ Change
        </button>
        <button onclick="decide('PROP-2026-06-30-001','DENY')"
          style="background:#7a1a1a;color:#fff;border:none;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;font-size:13px">
          ✗ Deny
        </button>
        <div id="status-PROP-2026-06-30-001" style="font-size:12px;color:#666;align-self:center;font-style:italic"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div id="bottombar">
  <div id="progress">0 / 21 reviewed</div>
  <div id="all-done">✓ All reviewed!</div>
  <button id="submit-btn" onclick="submitDecisions()">Send Decisions →</button>
</div>

<script>
const TOTAL = 21;
const decisions = {};
const notes = {};

function showCard(pid) {
  document.querySelectorAll('.prop-card').forEach(c => c.style.display='none');
  document.querySelectorAll('.sidebar-item').forEach(s => s.classList.remove('active'));
  document.getElementById('card-'+pid).style.display='block';
  document.getElementById('item-'+pid).classList.add('active');
  document.getElementById('welcome').style.display='none';
}

function decide(pid, decision) {
  decisions[pid] = decision;
  notes[pid] = notes[pid] || '';
  updateBadge(pid, decision);
  updateProgress();
  // Auto-advance to next pending
  const items = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.sidebar-item'));
  const cur = items.findIndex(el => el.id === 'item-'+pid);
  const next = items.slice(cur+1).find(el => !decisions[el.id.replace('item-','')]);
  if (next) showCard(next.id.replace('item-',''));
}

function decideChange(pid) {
  const note = prompt('What should be changed?\n(Your note will be sent with the decision)', '');
  if (note === null) return;
  decisions[pid] = 'CHANGE';
  notes[pid] = note;
  updateBadge(pid, 'CHANGE', note);
  updateProgress();
  const items = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.sidebar-item'));
  const cur = items.findIndex(el => el.id === 'item-'+pid);
  const next = items.slice(cur+1).find(el => !decisions[el.id.replace('item-','')]);
  if (next) showCard(next.id.replace('item-',''));
}

function updateBadge(pid, decision, note='') {
  const colors = {APPROVE:'#1a6b3c',CHECK:'#8a4a00',CHANGE:'#2a4a8a',DENY:'#7a1a1a'};
  const labels = {APPROVE:'✓ Approved',CHECK:'⚑ Flagged for check',CHANGE:'✎ Change requested',DENY:'✗ Denied'};
  const badge = document.getElementById('badge-'+pid);
  badge.style.color = colors[decision] || '#555';
  badge.style.fontWeight = '700';
  badge.textContent = labels[decision] + (note ? ' — '+note.slice(0,30) : '');
  const status = document.getElementById('status-'+pid);
  if (status) {
    status.style.color = colors[decision];
    status.textContent = labels[decision] + (note ? ': '+note : '');
  }
}

function updateProgress() {
  const done = Object.keys(decisions).length;
  document.getElementById('progress').textContent = done + ' / ' + TOTAL + ' reviewed';
  if (done >= TOTAL) {
    document.getElementById('all-done').style.display='inline';
    document.getElementById('submit-btn').style.background='#1a6b3c';
  }
}

function submitDecisions() {
  const lines = ['DECISIONS:'];
  const pids = ['PROP-2026-06-30-001', 'PROP-2026-06-30-002', 'PROP-2026-06-30-003', 'PROP-2026-06-30-004', 'PROP-2026-06-30-005', 'PROP-2026-06-30-006', 'PROP-2026-06-30-007', 'PROP-2026-06-30-008', 'PROP-2026-06-30-009', 'PROP-2026-06-30-010', 'PROP-2026-06-30-011', 'PROP-2026-06-30-012', 'PROP-2026-06-30-013', 'PROP-2026-06-30-014', 'PROP-2026-06-30-015', 'PROP-2026-06-30-016', 'PROP-2026-06-30-017', 'PROP-2026-06-30-018', 'PROP-2026-06-30-019', 'PROP-2026-06-30-020', 'PROP-2026-06-30-021'];
  pids.forEach(pid => {
    const d = decisions[pid] || 'PENDING';
    const n = notes[pid] ? ' | ' + notes[pid] : '';
    lines.push(pid + ': ' + d + n);
  });
  const body = lines.join('\n');
  const subject = '[C2A2-review-decision] 2026-06-30';
  // Open Gmail compose directly — avoids Apple Mail / default mail client
  const gmailUrl = 'https://mail.google.com/mail/?view=cm&fs=1&to=thomas.loughran@gmail.com&su='
    + encodeURIComponent(subject) + '&body=' + encodeURIComponent(body);
  window.open(gmailUrl, '_blank');
}

// Show first card on load
window.addEventListener('load', () => { showCard('PROP-2026-06-17-002'); });
</script>
</body>
</html>